<TopicPage slug="subjects/music/intermediate/scales-and-keys">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Scales are <strong>selected subsets of the twelve available notes, organised around a
    home note</strong> — the palette a piece of music paints from. Keys give scales a name
    and a centre; modulation moves between them. Underneath the theory sits real physics:
    an octave is a doubling of frequency, some intervals are simpler ratios than others,
    and every tuning system in history is a negotiation between those ratios and the
    freedom to change key. This page builds the system — octave, temperament, major and
    minor, chromatic, modes and pentatonic, then the wider world of rāga and maqām — and
    explains why choosing seven notes out of twelve turns out to be one of music's most
    consequential inventions. Keep one image as the
    spine: the tonic is a gravity well, scales are the routes into and out of it, and
    every scale system in the world is a way of deciding which routes are paved.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from twelve equal steps to the systems cultures built on them" widget="equationStep" config={keyCfg} />

  <h2>The octave and the twelve steps</h2>
  <p>
    Double a string's vibration frequency and you hear "the same note, higher" — the
    <strong>octave</strong>, ratio 2:1, the one interval every musical culture recognises
    as identity. Inside that doubling live the other consonant ratios: the fifth (3:2),
    the fourth (4:3), the major third (5:4). Tuning those pure ratios onto one chain of
    fifths, however, never closes the circle — twelve pure fifths overshoot seven octaves
    by a small but audible gap (the Pythagorean comma) — so something must give. The
    compromise that won is <strong>equal temperament</strong>: divide the octave into
    twelve exactly equal semitones, each of ratio 2^(1/12) ≈ 1.0595, so every interval is
    microscopically impure and every key is equally usable. Chinese prince and
    mathematician Zhu Zaiyu computed the division to nine decimal places in 1584; Europe
    converged on the same solution over the following century, and Bach's <em>Well-
    Tempered Clavier</em> (1722) — a prelude and fugue in every one of the 24 major and
    minor keys — is the monument to what the compromise bought: a keyboard that can play
    in any key without retuning.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> equal temperament is "the natural" tuning. It is a
    designed compromise — the pure fifth of a naturally buzzing string is about two cents
    flat of the tempered one, singers and string players still bend towards pure ratios
    in sustained chords, and non-Western systems (maqām, rāga, gamelan) divide the octave
    differently on purpose. Temperament is engineering, not nature; it optimises for
    modulation, and pays in purity.
  </p>

  <h2>Major and minor: the seven-note workhorses</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>major scale</strong> picks seven of the twelve semitones in a fixed pattern
    of steps — W-W-H-W-W-W-H (whole and half steps; or 2-2-1-2-2-2-1 semitones). Start the
    pattern on any of the twelve notes and you get one of the twelve major keys, each
    needing its own fixed sharps or flats — the <strong>key signature</strong> (C major has
    none; G major one sharp; F major one flat — the circle of fifths maps them all). A
    worked construction shows why the pattern is the whole theory: start on D, apply
    W-W-H-W-W-W-H, and you get D-E-F♯-G-A-B-C♯ — two sharps, so D major. Read a signature
    back the same way: for sharp keys, go a semitone above the last sharp (three sharps —
    F♯ C♯ G♯ — is A major); for flat keys, the second-to-last flat names the key
    (three flats — B♭ E♭ A♭ — is E♭ major). The
    <strong>minor scale</strong> lowers the 3rd, 6th and 7th degrees relative to its
    relative major (the scale sharing its key signature — A minor is C major's notes with
    a different home), and that one lowered third does enormous expressive work: Western
    listeners label the result "sad" instantly, whatever their training. Real minor-key
    music actually uses three versions — natural (for descending lines and modal colour),
    <strong>harmonic</strong> (raised 7th, creating the leading tone that pulls home, and
    the exotic augmented-second gap), and <strong>melodic</strong> (raised 6th and 7th
    ascending, natural descending) — because composers wanted the sad colour <em>and</em>
    the gravitational pull of a leading note. Keep relative and parallel straight with a
    worked pair: C major and A minor are <em>relative</em> minors (same signature, zero
    accidentals); C major and C minor are <em>parallel</em> (same tonic, three flats'
    difference — the darker sibling starting on the same home). Every pop ballad in A
    minor and every
    fugal subject in D minor is running on these choices.
  </p>

  <h2>Keys, the tonic and modulation</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>key</strong> is a scale with a home: the <strong>tonic</strong> works like
    gravity, and melodies end on it because arriving anywhere else leaves the ear leaning.
    Composers exploit that pull constantly — delaying the tonic, circling it, dodging it —
    and the biggest lever is <strong>modulation</strong>: changing key mid-piece. Modulate
    to the dominant (up a fifth) and the music opens outward; to the relative minor and a
    door closes; to a remote key and the floor shifts. Most modulation moves between
    <em>closely related</em> keys — those sharing most of a key signature — via a
    <strong>pivot chord</strong> that belongs to both worlds, and the listener hears not a
    rupture but a change of weather. The classical symphony was built on this drama (a
    first movement leaves home, a development wanders, the recapitulation returns),
    pop uses the lift of a chorus moved up a semitone, and film scoring states it
    outright: new key, new world. The canonical example is Beethoven's Fifth: the first
    movement is C minor — coiled, uncertain — and the finale answers in C major, same
    tonic, opposite weather; the whole symphony is an argument about which mode should
    own the note C. Tonality, in short, is a narrative technology.
  </p>

  <h2>Chromatic, modal, pentatonic</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>chromatic scale</strong> uses all twelve semitones — as a scale it is a
    ladder with no favourites, which is why it appears in music as colour (chromatic
    passing notes, sliding strings under a thriller) rather than as home. The
    <strong>modes</strong> are the older seven-note options: rotate the major scale's
    starting point and you get Dorian (minor with a bright raised 6th — "So What", folk
    fiddles), Phrygian (flat 2 — Spanish and metal), Mixolydian (flat 7 — rock, funk,
    bagpipes), Lydian (raised 4 — dreamlike film cues) and more; the names are borrowed
    from ancient Greek tribes via medieval theorists, and no mode uses any note the
    keyboard cannot play — they are rotations, not aliens. The modes predate
    major/minor, ruled medieval and Renaissance music, and were revived by jazz and film
    because each has a distinct face. The <strong>pentatonic scale</strong> — five notes,
    usually the major scale minus its two most awkward steps — is the nearest thing
    music has to a universal: it names itself in Chinese <em>gongche</em> and Japanese
    <em>in sen</em> traditions, Scottish folk, Indonesian slendro, blues guitar, gospel,
    children's songs and the black keys of the piano. Its secret is safety: with no
    half-step neighbours, any ordering of its notes sounds consonant — which is why
    beginners improvise freely on it and why a blues solo never seems to miss. Verify
    it at the keyboard: play any group of black keys in any order and the result is
    pentatonic music, instantly singable — five carefully chosen notes out of twelve
    that cannot collide. The minor pentatonic (the same five notes recast from the minor
    scale) plus one "blue note" is the entire vocabulary of a thousand blues solos —
    small palette, vast repertoire.
  </p>

  <h2>Rāga and maqām: different answers to the same question</h2>
  <p>
    Two great systems organise pitch without Western chords. An Indian <strong>rāga</strong>
    is not a scale but a melodic being-with-rules: a set of notes (five, six or seven,
    from either a 12-semitone or finer division), a required ascent and descent, signature
    phrases, ornaments, an emotional character (<em>rasa</em>) and often an assigned time
    of day; performance unfolds over a drone (tanpura) with no harmony except the melody's
    own shadow — depth without chord change. A morning rāga like Bhairavi and an evening
    rāga like Yaman share notes with Western scales yet occupy different expressive
    worlds, because the rules of movement, not just the collection of notes, carry the
    meaning. Arabic <strong>maqām</strong> similarly names
    melodic territories built from small cells (<em>ajnās</em>) that commonly use
    <strong>microtones</strong> — intervals of roughly quarter-tones lying between
    Western semitones — tuned by ear on unfretted instruments (oud, violin) and fixed
    by levers on the qanun; maqām Rast's "E half-flat" is a real pitch with a real
    emotional address that no Western key signature can spell. Both systems treat pitch
    as a landscape with customs, not
    just a palette, and both expose the limit of staff notation: a Western page can only
    approximate what maqām Rast or rāga Bhairavi actually ask for — the sound the
    notation struggles with is often the most local and most expressive part. A shared
    lesson from the drone traditions (Indian, Scottish piobaireachd, gamelan): music can
    organise an entire art around a single sustained reference pitch, which suggests the
    "home note" idea is deeper than the chord progressions built on top of it.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The physics behind the feelings is worth one honest paragraph. Consonance tracks
    simplicity of ratio: an octave (2:1) or fifth (3:2) overlaps neatly in the ear's
    critical bands, while more complex ratios (the tritone, 45:32 in just tuning)
    produce <strong>beating</strong> — a roughness the auditory system registers as
    tension. Equal temperament's major third (2^(4/12) ≈ 1.2599) is measurably wider
    than the pure 5:4 (1.25), which is why choirs a cappella drift sharp on sustained
    major chords: ears want the pure ratio even when instruments deny it. This also
    explains blue notes: blues flattens the 3rd and 7th into the gap <em>between</em>
    the fixed keys — expressive zones the 12-note grid only approximates — and explains
    why "minor sounds sad" deserves a caveat: the acoustic signature is real (a
    lower third changes the overtone story), but the emotional label is learned
    (some musics use "minor-ish" material for celebration, and 19th-century minor-key
    dances do not weep). The remaining open question is where tonality itself comes
    from — innate psychoacoustics or lifelong exposure — and the honest answer is
    both: the octave looks universal, the rest is culture with very good physics.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two last refinements. History runs deeper than 1584: the Greeks theorised
    <em>genera</em> and modes two millennia ago, medieval church modes governed European
    chant, and meantone temperaments sweetened the common keys at the expense of the
    remote ones long before equal temperament standardised the compromise — Bach's title
    (<em>wohltemperirt</em>, "well tempered") probably names one of those pragmatic
    systems rather than modern equal tuning. And the modern frontier is electronic:
    synthesizers and microtonal plugins now make any division of the octave playable,
    from 19-tone to 53-tone scales, so the old engineering constraint (fixed keyboards)
    that drove temperament history has quietly dissolved — what remains is the question
    equal temperament answered so well: which compromises are worth the music they
    enable?
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Benson, Music: A Mathematical Offering; Isacoff, Temperament; Huron, Sweet Anticipation; Indian raga and Arabic maqam theory sources.</p>
